{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgQVAWBNLKddR2PMRAPYH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I ruled F1 SUCCESSOR. The verdict is at `docs/reviews/wi-976/021-ADJUDICATE-095bb79.md`, committed once as a9be83f29 with `WI: WI-976` and `Loop-Session: 20261025T055732Z-64b2b6` in one trailer block.\n\n**Conflict of interest:** this session wrote the record under judgement, sitting 016's verdict, and the earlier verdicts the finding cites (011, 012, 015). I agree with the finding, so the ruling doesn't favour its author. The verdict says this plainly, in case you want a different hand on any re-inspection.\n\n**`RULING: F1 SUCCESSOR`**\n\n**The finding is real.** Sitting 016's brief (lines 644–649) asks the verdict to record why further splitting stops, and the verdict has no such sentence; neither do 011, 012 or 015. The fresh TC-211 inspection recorded that as a fail, while finding no child without a purpose. What's missing is the written reason, not a sound decomposition.\n\n**It isn't convergence-class:**\n- Nothing gates on the record, and nothing is lost.\n- Every TC-211 item in the Done-when holds, including the fresh inspection and its recorded result. The Done-when asks for that result, not for a pass.\n- The clause that failed is SR-186's stopping-boundary criterion, which this lane doesn't change. SR-161's part, every hat's outcome named, passes.\n\n**Why not FIX here:** the omission is systemic. Four verdicts against the same instruction all left it out, because the verdict format (row lines, HAT lines, machine line) has no place for a stopping reason. One more re-sit would turn this sample green and leave the next round free to drop the reason the same way. The real fix gives the stopping boundary a named line in the shipped sitting brief. That changes every adopter's briefs and belongs in its own row.\n\n**What the successor row must do:** add a named stopping-boundary line beside the hat outcome lines, test that the shipped templates carry it, and re-inspect TC-211 on the first round whose record has it. Until then the fail stands as the observation of record, and the release trigger will ask for a passing sample.\n\nI also saved a memory: in decomposition-round verdicts, state per chain why splitting stops, so my next sittings don't repeat the omission before the successor lands.\n\nThe commit hooks passed, including `held-status`, with five readability worsenings in measures that aren't gating. I left the untracked `.requested` marker alone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":317,"cache_read_input_tokens":826376,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":317},"output_tokens":721,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f031c6a5-ecf0-490f-9890-c7a8a87f92fc","uuid":"826dee9a-ad4c-40ea-9a49-c3248e3b0641","timestamp":"2026-10-26T07:53:01.297Z","request_id":"req_011CgQVAVqHJVUcjs3ZaiyEG"}
